  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery indicates a deepening upper low digging into the \nPacific Northwest, with strong upper ridging dominating much of the \nrest of the CONUS. Elsewhere, Hurricane Isaias is nearing landfall \non the Florida Panhandle. All the latent heat release with Isaias is \nhelping to strengthen the ridging over the Midwest and Great Lakes, \namplifying and slowing the overall pattern. So we will continue to \nsee hot and dry weather through the weekend as the upper low deepens \nand mostly becomes cut off over the southwestern CONUS. Highs \napproach 90 each afternoon with overnight lows mainly in the 60s. \nMonday still looks to be the warmest day as WAA and moisture \nadvection strengthen in advance of the upper low as it begins to \nshift east. Highs in the low 90s and lows near 70 will both be near \nrecord values. (See Climate section.)\n\nTuesday into Thursday, uncertainty significantly increases. High \npressure over the Northern Plains will push a cold front southward \ntowards the area Monday night and Tuesday. There is significant \nspread in guidance in how far south this front will get before it \nstalls. Behind the front, temperatures should stay in the upper 60s \nand 70s, while ahead of the front will be another very warm day near \nor above 90. A few stronger storms could also occur along and ahead \nof the front as it presses southeast. By Wednesday and Thursday, the \nfront will lift back north as a warm front as the Southwest upper \nlow moves northeast across the Plains. High uncertainty is also \npresent as to how this system will evolve. Some recent guidance \nkeeps the upper low a lot weaker and slower as a northern stream \nshortwave over western Canada fails to dig southeast and interact \nwith the original upper low. Other guidance still has a stronger and \nmore negatively tilted trough that would be more favorable for some \nsort of regional severe threat. At any rate, we should see rain and \nstorm chances increase whenever the system does shift east across \nthe area. Just tough to pinpoint any specific area or time given the \ncurrent uncertainty. Behind the system, still expecting cooler \ntemperatures to arrive for the end of next week as high pressure \nmoves across the Midwest. The degree of that cooler air is still in \nquestion though, given the aforementioned uncertainty in the overall \npattern evolution.",
